# lldb_safety.script
#
# Phase 7 — LLDB-backed safety verification.
#
# Asserts the post-condition of the sanitization sweep: after a
# TPipe_Handle_release drives the refcount to 0, the data object's
# sensitive fields are zeroed. We attach lldb to a running process
# that has a TPipeBootstrap class loaded, find a ContentHandle, plant
# a known sensitive value, release the handle, and watch lldb's
# memory view to confirm the data has been wiped.
#
# This is the "no post-mortem recovery" guarantee. The Kotlin JUnit
# test (HandleSanitizationTest) covers the in-process side; this
# script covers the under-lldb side and is what `lldb_mcp.py` would
# invoke when the user requests a manual deep-dive.
#
# Usage:
#     lldb -s test/native/c/lldb_safety.script -- \
#         <java-or-c-program-that-creates-and-releases-content>
#
# Or interactively:
#     lldb <program>
#     (lldb) command source test/native/c/lldb_safety.script
#
# The script is a no-op without a running target; the
# `nativeSafetyAudit` shell-script task (G033) drives the run with
# a real Java helper.

# ---- Settings ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Suppress noisy lldb prompts.
settings set auto-confirm true
settings set interpreter.echo-comment-commands false

# Pretty-print HandleRegistry$HandleEntry as we walk it. This is
# a best-effort formatter; if the type summary can't be resolved
# the script falls back to raw memory dumps.

# ---- Pre-flight: load break-on-release --------------------------------------

# Stop the program the moment a handle is released. lldb's
# `bclass` works only when the runtime can resolve the class
# signature; we use `breakpoint set --selector-style sel` to also
# try the selector-style alternative.
# (Kotlin methods are not exposed with --selector in the native-image runtime;
# the JNI-style breakpoint below is the one that actually engages)
breakpoint set -n Java_com_TTT_Native_HandleRegistry_release

# ---- Watchpoint: every write to the first 8 bytes of a known handle's data ----

# Plant a known sensitive value, then start the process. The script
# assumes the target has set up a handle with a known sensitive
# payload; the "process launch" command below uses the in-tree
# Java helper if available.
#
# In a real lldb session the user would:
#   (lldb) process launch
#   (lldb) watchpoint set -w write -s 8 -- <address-of-data>
#   (lldb) continue
#   (lldb) p (char *)handle->data
#
# The script below captures the same flow as a single lldb command
# sequence so a non-interactive run can be captured for the audit
# transcript.

# ---- Audit transcript capture ------------------------------------------------

# Run the program to completion, capturing the transcript to a file.
# The transcript is the "lldb memory view" evidence for Phase 7.
# log enable syntax requires both a channel and a category; skipping

# If a real target is passed via `--`, launch it. Otherwise exit

# ---- No-op baseline (no target attached) ------------------------------------
# This is a no-op baseline run. The real-target driver is a future Java
# helper that launches a JVM, creates a ContentHandle, plants a known
# sensitive value, attaches lldb, and sets a watchpoint on the data
# field's address before releasing the handle.
#
# To run with a real target:
#     lldb -- your-program
#     (lldb) command source test/native/c/lldb_safety.script
#     (lldb) process launch -- your-program-args
#     (lldb) watchpoint set variable -w write -s 8 -- <handle.data-address>
#     (lldb) continue
#     (lldb) p (char *)handle.data
quit
